  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL: The claim 'No extant work combines probe-based internal-state measurement with causal-interventional measurement on the same instances' is FALSE. 'LLMs Faithfully and Iteratively Compute Answers During CoT' (2412.01113, EACL 2026) trains linear probes AND performs activation patching on the same instances to study CoT faithfulness. 'Bypassing the Rationale' (2602.03994) combines activation patching with probing to produce the CoT Mediation Index (CMI). The landscape section must acknowledge these and reposition the novelty.",
    "CRITICAL: 'Hidden Error Awareness in Chain-of-Thought Reasoning: The Signal Is Diagnostic, Not Causal' (2605.09502, May 12 2026) shows that probes detect error awareness at 0.95 AUROC but four intervention methods (activation steering, probe-guided best-of-N, self-correction, activation patching) ALL FAIL. The paper concludes 'error representations during reasoning are fundamentally different from editable factual knowledge representations.' This directly challenges the interpretive foundation of F(t) = P(t) − C(t): high P(t) may not mean the model 'knows' the answer in a causally relevant sense — the gap may simply reflect the non-causal nature of probe-detected information, not unfaithfulness.",
    "MISSING REFERENCE: 2412.01113 'LLMs Faithfully and Iteratively Compute Answers During CoT' (EACL 2026) — combines probing + activation patching on same instances for CoT faithfulness. Crucially, this paper finds CoT IS faithful (for arithmetic), providing a counterpoint to the proposal's focus on unfaithfulness.",
    "MISSING REFERENCE: 2602.03994 'Bypassing the Rationale: Causal Auditing of Implicit Reasoning' — introduces CoT Mediation Index (CMI) via activation patching + probing, identifies 'bypass regimes' where CMI ≈ 0 despite plausible CoT.",
    "MISSING REFERENCE: 2604.10693 'FACT-E: Causality-Inspired Evaluation for Trustworthy Chain-of-Thought Reasoning' (ACL Findings 2026) — causality-inspired framework measuring intra-chain faithfulness + CoT-to-answer consistency via controlled perturbations.",
